/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/az-104.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("az-104", [["adm1",0,1,"A new employee in Marketing must automatically join the Marketing-All group when HR sets their department attribute. What should the administrator configure?",["An assigned security group with an owner who adds each new user","A Microsoft 365 group with guest access enabled","A dynamic user group with a rule on the department attribute","An administrative unit scoped to the Marketing department"],2,"Dynamic user groups add and remove members automatically based on attribute rules. An assigned group needs someone to add members, and an administrative unit only scopes admin permissions.","Manage Entra users and groups",["An assigned group only changes when someone adds or removes members by hand, so new Marketing hires would not join automatically.","Guest access controls whether external users can join a Microsoft 365 group; it does nothing to add members based on the department attribute.",null,"Administrative units scope who can administer a set of users; they do not add users to groups."]],["adm2",0,1,"A partner's consultant needs to sign in with their own company account to access one resource group in your tenant. What should you do?",["Create a new cloud-only user account in your tenant for the consultant","Invite the consultant as a B2B guest user and assign an RBAC role","Share the resource group's access keys with the consultant","Add the partner's domain as a custom domain in your tenant"],1,"B2B collaboration lets external users sign in with their home identity; you then grant access with RBAC. A separate account duplicates identities, and resource groups have no access keys.","Manage external users",["A separate cloud-only account makes the consultant manage a second password and leaves an identity you must deprovision yourself, instead of letting them use their own company account.",null,"Resource groups have no access keys; keys belong to specific services such as storage accounts, and sharing them bypasses identity-based access.","Adding a custom domain lets your own tenant use that domain name for its users; it does not let accounts from another organization sign in."]],["adm3",0,1,"A team lead must create and manage all resources in a resource group but must not be able to grant anyone else access. Which built-in role fits?",["Owner","User Access Administrator","Reader","Contributor"],3,"Contributor can manage all resources but cannot assign roles. Owner can also assign roles, and User Access Administrator manages access without managing resources.","Manage access to Azure resources",["Owner includes Microsoft.Authorization write rights, so the team lead could assign roles to others, which the requirement forbids.","User Access Administrator manages role assignments but cannot create or manage resources, which is the opposite of what is needed.","Reader can only view resources, so the team lead could not create or change anything.",null]],["adm4",0,1,"A help desk engineer must manage role assignments on a subscription but should not create or modify resources. Which role is the best fit?",["User Access Administrator","Owner","Contributor","Security Reader"],0,"User Access Administrator manages access to resources without resource management rights. Owner would also allow resource changes, which breaks least privilege.","Manage access to Azure resources",[null,"Owner can manage role assignments but also create, change and delete resources, which breaks least privilege here.","Contributor can manage resources but cannot manage role assignments, so it fails both parts of the requirement.","Security Reader is a read-only role for security data in Defender for Cloud; it cannot manage role assignments."]],["adm5",0,1,"A user is assigned Reader at the subscription and Contributor on resource group RG1. What can they do in RG1?",["Only view resources, because the subscription-level role takes priority","Nothing, because conflicting assignments cancel out","Create and modify resources, because RBAC permissions are additive","View resources only until an Owner approves the Contributor role"],2,"Azure RBAC is additive: effective permissions are the union of all assignments at the scope and above, so Contributor applies in RG1. Assignments do not cancel each other.","Interpret access assignments",["Azure RBAC has no priority between scopes; a broader Reader assignment does not cap a narrower Contributor assignment.","Role assignments never cancel out; only a deny assignment can remove permissions that a role grants.",null,"Built-in role assignments take effect immediately; approval workflows exist only if you add something like PIM, which the question does not mention."]],["adm6",0,1,"An auditor needs to read blob contents in one storage account but must not manage the account. Which role should you assign on the account?",["Reader","Storage Blob Data Reader","Storage Account Contributor","Contributor"],1,"Storage Blob Data Reader grants data-plane read access to blobs. Reader only covers the management plane and cannot read data with Entra authentication, and the contributor roles are far too broad.","Manage access to Azure resources",["Reader covers the management plane only, so it shows the account's settings but does not grant data actions to read blob contents with Entra ID.",null,"Storage Account Contributor manages the account, including its keys, which is far more than read access and gives management rights the auditor must not have.","Contributor can manage every resource in its scope, including listing keys, which is much broader than reading blobs."]],["adm7",0,1,"Company policy says resources may only be created in East US and West Europe, even by subscription Owners. What should you implement?",["A custom RBAC role without write permissions in other regions","A ReadOnly lock on the subscription","A budget with an alert on other regions","An Azure Policy assignment using Allowed locations"],3,"Azure Policy with the Allowed locations definition denies deployments to other regions regardless of the deployer's role. RBAC controls actions, not locations, and a lock blocks every change.","Implement and manage Azure Policy",["RBAC permissions describe actions, not regions, so a role cannot allow a write in East US but deny the same write elsewhere; Owners would also still hold their own role.","A ReadOnly lock blocks all changes to the subscription's resources, including deployments to the allowed regions.","A budget only tracks cost and sends alerts; it never blocks a deployment in any region.",null]],["adm8",0,1,"You assigned a policy with the Modify effect that adds a CostCenter tag. New resources get the tag, but existing ones do not. What should you do?",["Create a remediation task for the policy assignment","Reassign the policy with the Audit effect","Apply a CanNotDelete lock on the resource group","Add the tag to the subscription so it inherits down"],0,"Modify and DeployIfNotExists only act on new or updated resources until you run a remediation task, which uses the assignment's managed identity. Tags on a subscription are not inherited.","Implement and manage Azure Policy",[null,"Audit only reports non-compliance; switching to it would stop adding the tag rather than fix existing resources.","A CanNotDelete lock only prevents deletion and has nothing to do with adding tags.","Tags are not inherited from a subscription or resource group, so tagging the subscription does not change the resources."]],["adm9",0,1,"Which statement about Azure resource tags is correct?",["Tags on a resource group are automatically copied to all of its resources","Tags can only be applied to resources, never to resource groups","Resources do not inherit resource group tags unless a policy applies them","Tags set on a subscription are enforced on all resources under it"],2,"Tags are not inherited. To copy a resource group tag to resources, use a policy such as Inherit a tag from the resource group. Resource groups and subscriptions can have their own tags.","Manage tags",["Resources do not inherit resource group tags automatically; you need a policy such as Inherit a tag from the resource group.","Resource groups and subscriptions can have their own tags, so this restriction is not true.",null,"Subscription tags are not inherited or enforced on child resources; enforcement needs Azure Policy."]],["adm10",0,1,"A production resource group must never be deleted by accident, but admins must still update resources in it. Which lock should be applied?",["ReadOnly","CanNotDelete","Deny assignment","A Deny policy for deletes"],1,"CanNotDelete blocks deletion but allows reading and modifying. ReadOnly would block updates as well, and a Deny policy evaluates resource properties rather than delete operations (Azure Policy's newer DenyAction effect can block deletes, but a CanNotDelete lock is the direct, simplest control).","Configure resource locks",["ReadOnly blocks every write operation, so admins could no longer update resources in the group.",null,"Deny assignments are created by Azure features such as managed applications and deployment stacks; you cannot create one directly as a simple accidental-delete guard.","The Deny effect evaluates create and update requests, not deletes; only the newer DenyAction effect can block deletes, and a lock is the simpler built-in answer."]],["adm11",0,1,"After an admin applied a ReadOnly lock to a storage account, an app that lists the account keys began to fail. Why?",["ReadOnly locks also revoke existing SAS tokens","ReadOnly locks force the account to Archive tier","Listing keys requires the Owner role, which the lock removes","Listing keys is a POST operation, which ReadOnly blocks"],3,"ReadOnly blocks any write-type operation, and listing keys uses a POST request, so it fails. The lock does not change roles, tiers or SAS tokens.","Configure resource locks",["Locks do not touch SAS tokens; existing SAS tokens keep working until they expire or their signing key is rotated.","Locks never change a storage account's access tier, and Archive cannot be set as an account default tier anyway.","Locks do not add or remove role assignments; listing keys requires the listKeys action, not specifically Owner.",null]],["adm12",0,1,"An organization with 40 subscriptions wants to apply the same policies and RBAC assignments to groups of subscriptions by department. What should it use?",["Management groups","Resource groups","Administrative units","Azure Blueprints artifacts on each resource"],0,"Management groups sit above subscriptions, and policies and role assignments applied there are inherited by every subscription beneath. Resource groups sit below subscriptions.","Manage subscriptions and management groups",[null,"Resource groups sit inside a single subscription, so they cannot group whole subscriptions by department.","Administrative units scope Entra ID admin permissions over users, groups and devices; they do not hold Azure subscriptions or policies.","Blueprints are deprecated, and applying artifacts to each resource individually is the opposite of central, inherited assignment."]],["adm13",0,1,"A team wants an email when their subscription's monthly spending forecast passes 80% of a set amount. What should be configured?",["An activity log alert on resource write operations in the subscription","A Cost Management budget with a forecasted alert threshold","An Azure Advisor recommendation digest","A resource lock on the subscription"],1,"Budgets in Cost Management can alert on actual or forecasted cost at thresholds such as 80%. Advisor digests summarize recommendations, not spending against a limit.","Manage costs",["Activity log alerts fire on operations such as writes or deletes, not on spending thresholds or forecasts.",null,"Advisor digests summarize recommendations on a schedule; they do not compare spending with an amount you set.","A lock controls delete or write operations and has no cost or notification function."]],["adm14",0,1,"You must move a VM and its disks and NIC from RG-Dev to RG-Prod in the same subscription. What is true?",["The VM must be recreated from a snapshot in the new resource group","Resources keep their region; move dependent resources together","The move changes the VM's region to match RG-Prod","Moving resource groups is only possible with ARM templates"],1,"A resource group move changes only the group, not the region, and a VM should move with its dependent disks and NICs. Resources are locked for writes during the move, but nothing is recreated.","Manage resource groups",["Moving between resource groups does not recreate the VM; Azure moves the existing resources and their IDs change to reflect the new group.",null,"A resource's region is fixed when it is created; a resource group move never changes it, and the group's own location is only metadata.","You can move resources between groups from the portal, Azure CLI, PowerShell or the REST API; templates are not required."]],["adm15",0,1,"Users want to reset their own forgotten passwords without calling the help desk. What should you enable?",["Self-service password reset for a group or all users","Conditional Access that requires a compliant or hybrid-joined device","Privileged Identity Management for all users","A password expiration policy of 30 days"],0,"Self-service password reset lets registered users reset their password with methods such as the Authenticator app or phone. Conditional Access and PIM do not reset passwords.","Configure self-service password reset",[null,"Conditional Access decides when and how users can sign in; it does not let users reset a forgotten password.","Privileged Identity Management provides just-in-time activation of privileged roles; it is not a password reset tool.","Forcing more frequent expiration makes forgotten passwords more likely and still leaves resets to the help desk."]],["adm16",0,1,"You want every member of the Sales group to receive a Microsoft 365 license automatically, including future members. What should you do?",["Assign licenses to each current user through a scheduled PowerShell script","Add the Sales group as owner of the license","Create a budget scoped to the Sales group","Assign the license to the Sales group using group-based licensing"],3,"Group-based licensing assigns the license to everyone in the group and follows membership changes. A script only covers current members, and licenses have no owners.","Manage licenses",["A scheduled script only licenses users who exist at run time and adds maintenance; group-based licensing follows membership changes on its own.","Licenses do not have owners, so making a group an owner of a license is not a real configuration.","Budgets track Azure spending and cannot be scoped to an Entra group or assign licenses.",null]],["adm17",0,1,"A developer needs to create 500 user accounts in Microsoft Entra ID from a spreadsheet. What is the simplest built-in method?",["Invite each user through B2B collaboration from a partner tenant","Create a dynamic group with 500 members","Use Bulk create with the downloaded CSV template","Create a management group for the new users"],2,"The Entra admin center offers Bulk create with a CSV template. B2B invites are for external users, and dynamic groups manage membership, not accounts.","Manage Entra users and groups",["B2B invitations create guest accounts for people from other organizations, not 500 new member accounts in your tenant.","A dynamic group only manages membership of accounts that already exist; it cannot create users.",null,"Management groups organize Azure subscriptions; they have nothing to do with creating Entra user accounts."]],["adm18",0,1,"A policy definition needs to create a diagnostic setting on any Key Vault that lacks one. Which effect is required?",["Audit","DeployIfNotExists","Deny","Append"],1,"DeployIfNotExists deploys a related resource (such as a diagnostic setting) when it is missing. Audit only reports, Deny blocks the request and Append adds fields to the request.","Implement and manage Azure Policy",["Audit only marks resources as non-compliant; it never creates the missing diagnostic setting.",null,"Deny blocks non-compliant requests but cannot create a related resource on an existing Key Vault.","Append adds fields to the resource being created or updated; it cannot deploy a separate resource such as a diagnostic setting."]],["adm19",0,1,"Several related policy definitions must be assigned together and tracked as one compliance score. What should you create?",["A management group","A policy exemption","A custom RBAC role","An initiative (policy set definition)"],3,"An initiative groups policy definitions into one assignment with a combined compliance view. An exemption removes resources from evaluation instead.","Implement and manage Azure Policy",["A management group is a scope where you assign policies, not a way to bundle definitions into one compliance score.","A policy exemption removes a scope from evaluation, which is the opposite of grouping definitions for tracking.","A custom RBAC role defines permissions for actions; it does not group policy definitions or report compliance.",null]],["adm20",0,1,"Azure Advisor shows several VMs with very low CPU use over two weeks. Which kind of recommendation is this and what is the usual action?",["A cost recommendation to resize or shut down underused VMs","A security recommendation to enable just-in-time VM access on each VM","A reliability recommendation to add availability zones","An operational excellence recommendation to add tags"],0,"Advisor cost recommendations flag underused VMs so you can right-size or shut them down. Security, reliability and operational recommendations address different issues.","Manage costs",[null,"Just-in-time VM access is a Defender for Cloud control for management ports; it does not address low CPU use.","Availability zones improve resilience; they do not fix VMs that are oversized for their load.","Tags help organize resources, but low CPU use is flagged as a cost opportunity, not an operational excellence issue."]],["adm21",0,1,"Which Entra ID group type can be used to assign Azure RBAC roles to its members?",["A Microsoft 365 group with dynamic device membership rules","Distribution list synchronized from Exchange","A security group or a security-enabled Microsoft 365 group","Any group type, including mail-enabled distribution lists"],2,"Role assignments need security principals: security groups, and security-enabled Microsoft 365 groups. Distribution lists are not security principals.","Manage Entra users and groups",["Microsoft 365 groups cannot use dynamic device membership at all (only security groups can), and what makes a group usable for RBAC is being security-enabled, not its rule.","Distribution lists are mail-only objects, not security principals, so they cannot receive Azure role assignments.",null,"Mail-enabled distribution lists are not security principals, so not every group type can be used for RBAC."]],["adm22",0,1,"An admin gave a user Contributor on a subscription, but the user cannot see a resource in RG1 that has a deny assignment for that user. What explains this?",["Contributor does not include read permissions on resources","Deny assignments take priority over role assignments","The user needs the Owner role to override a deny assignment","Deny assignments only apply to Entra roles"],1,"Deny assignments block actions even when a role assignment allows them. Owner does not override them, and Contributor includes read permissions.","Interpret access assignments",["Contributor includes read permissions on all resources in its scope, so missing reads are not the cause.",null,"Owner does not override deny assignments; deny assignments block the listed actions even for Owners.","Deny assignments apply to Azure resource (RBAC) permissions, not to Entra ID directory roles."]],["adm23",0,2,"A storage account must survive the loss of one datacenter within the region but data must never leave the region. Which redundancy should you choose?",["LRS","GRS","ZRS","RA-GRS"],2,"ZRS keeps three copies across availability zones in the same region. LRS stays in one datacenter, and GRS or RA-GRS copy data to a paired region.","Configure redundancy",["LRS keeps all three copies in a single datacenter, so losing that datacenter can make the data unavailable.","GRS copies data asynchronously to the paired region, which breaks the rule that data must never leave the region.",null,"RA-GRS also replicates to the paired region (and adds read access there), so data leaves the region."]],["adm24",0,2,"An app must keep reading blob data from a secondary region while the primary region is unavailable, with zone resilience in the primary. Which option fits?",["RA-GZRS","GRS","ZRS","LRS"],0,"RA-GZRS uses zones in the primary region and lets clients read from the secondary endpoint. GRS copies to the secondary but reads there only after a failover.","Configure redundancy",[null,"GRS keeps the primary copies in one datacenter (no zones) and the secondary is readable only after a failover.","ZRS is zone-resilient but has no secondary region, so there is nothing to read from during a regional outage.","LRS has neither zone redundancy nor a secondary region."]],["adm25",0,2,"A contractor needs to upload files to one container for the next 8 hours only. You want the most secure SAS that does not use the account key. Which should you issue?",["An account SAS with write permission for all services","A service SAS signed with key1","The storage account's connection string","A user delegation SAS scoped to the container"],3,"A user delegation SAS is signed with Entra credentials instead of an account key and can be limited to the container and 8 hours. A connection string gives full account access.","Configure access to storage",["An account SAS is signed with an account key and here grants write to every service, far broader than one container.","A service SAS signed with key1 depends on the account key, which the requirement says to avoid, and is less secure than a user delegation SAS.","A connection string contains the account key and grants full control of the whole account with no expiry.",null]],["adm26",0,2,"You issued service SAS tokens for a container and must be able to revoke them all without rotating account keys. What should you have used?",["A stored access policy referenced by the tokens","An IP address range in each SAS token","A ReadOnly lock on the container","Blob versioning on the account"],0,"Service SAS tokens linked to a stored access policy can be revoked by changing or deleting the policy. Without it, only key rotation or expiry ends them.","Configure access to storage",[null,"An IP range limits where a token can be used from but gives you no way to revoke tokens that are already issued.","A lock protects the container resource on the management plane; it does not invalidate SAS tokens for data access.","Versioning keeps previous blob versions; it has no effect on whether a SAS token is still valid."]],["adm27",0,2,"A storage account must be reachable only from one subnet in your VNet and from Azure Backup, with the public endpoint still in use. What should you configure?",["A private endpoint in the subnet with public network access disabled","Storage firewall VNet rule plus the trusted services exception","A network security group on the storage account","A SAS token restricted to the subnet's IP range"],1,"Storage firewall VNet rules (with a service endpoint on the subnet) limit the public endpoint to that subnet, and the trusted Microsoft services exception lets Azure Backup through. NSGs cannot attach to storage accounts.","Configure storage firewalls and virtual networks",["A private endpoint with public access disabled removes the public endpoint, but the requirement says the public endpoint stays in use.",null,"NSGs attach to subnets and NICs, not to storage accounts, so one cannot filter access to the account.","SAS tokens can restrict public IP ranges, but VNet traffic reaches storage from private addresses, and a SAS does not stop other clients from using keys."]],["adm28",0,2,"Security requires that storage encryption keys be controlled and rotatable by the company. What should you configure?",["Infrastructure encryption only","Microsoft-managed keys with key rotation","Customer-managed keys stored in Azure Key Vault","An encryption scope that uses Microsoft-managed keys"],2,"Customer-managed keys in Key Vault or Managed HSM put key control and rotation with the customer. Microsoft-managed keys are rotated by Microsoft.","Configure encryption",["Infrastructure encryption adds a second layer with Microsoft-managed keys; it does not give the company control of the keys.","With Microsoft-managed keys Microsoft owns and rotates the keys, so the company does not control them.",null,"An encryption scope that uses Microsoft-managed keys still leaves key control with Microsoft."]],["adm29",0,2,"A team wants double encryption at rest for a new storage account. When must infrastructure encryption be enabled?",["At any time from the Encryption blade","Only after customer-managed keys are set up","After the account is converted to GRS","When the storage account is created"],3,"Infrastructure encryption must be enabled when the account is created and cannot be turned on later. It does not depend on redundancy or on customer-managed keys.","Configure encryption",["Infrastructure encryption cannot be switched on later from the Encryption blade; it is fixed when the account is created.","Infrastructure encryption does not depend on customer-managed keys and must be chosen at creation, not afterwards.","Changing redundancy has nothing to do with infrastructure encryption, which can only be set at creation.",null]],["adm30",0,2,"Log files are read often for 30 days, rarely for a year, then must be deleted. How can you manage this automatically at the lowest cost?",["A lifecycle rule: Cool after 30 days, delete after 365 days","A scheduled Azure Function that copies old blobs to a second account","Setting the account default tier to Archive","Enabling blob soft delete with a 365-day retention"],0,"Lifecycle management tiers and deletes blobs by age with no code. Archive as a default is not supported at account level, and soft delete keeps deleted data rather than removing it.","Configure Blob Storage",[null,"A custom Function adds code to maintain and a second account to pay for, while lifecycle management does the same with no code.","Archive cannot be the account's default tier, and archived logs would be offline during the 30 days they are read often.","Soft delete keeps deleted blobs recoverable; it does not delete old data or move it to cheaper tiers."]],["adm31",0,2,"A blob in the Archive tier is needed urgently for an audit. What must happen before it can be read?",["Nothing; Archive blobs can be read directly, only with higher latency","It must be rehydrated to an online tier such as Hot or Cool","It must be copied with AzCopy to a file share","The account must be converted to Premium"],1,"Archive is offline storage. The blob must be rehydrated to Hot, Cool or Cold (or copied to an online blob), which can take hours; high priority can be faster for small blobs.","Configure Blob Storage",["Archive blobs are offline, so they cannot be read at all until they are rehydrated or copied to an online tier.",null,"AzCopy cannot read an archived blob's data either, and copying to Azure Files is not how rehydration works.","Premium performance is a separate account type; converting the account does not bring archived blobs online."]],["adm32",0,2,"You must replicate block blobs from a container in one account to a container in another region's account asynchronously. What must be enabled first?",["Soft delete and point-in-time restore on both accounts","Hierarchical namespace on the destination account","Premium performance on the source","Versioning on both accounts, change feed on the source"],3,"Object replication requires versioning on both source and destination and change feed on the source. Hierarchical namespace accounts are not supported for object replication.","Configure object replication",["Soft delete and point-in-time restore protect data within an account; they are not prerequisites for object replication.","Accounts with hierarchical namespace are not supported for object replication, so enabling it would block the setup.","Object replication works with standard general-purpose v2 accounts; Premium performance is not required.",null]],["adm33",0,2,"A user accidentally overwrote an important blob. Which feature would let you restore the previous content?",["Container soft delete","Blob versioning","Storage firewall","Access tier set to Cool"],1,"Versioning keeps previous versions each time a blob is overwritten. Container soft delete protects against deleting a whole container, not overwrites.","Configure data protection",["Container soft delete restores a whole deleted container, not the previous content of a blob that was overwritten.",null,"The storage firewall controls which networks can reach the account and cannot recover overwritten data.","The Cool tier lowers storage cost but keeps no earlier copies of a blob."]],["adm34",0,2,"You need to copy 2 TB of files from an on-premises server to Blob Storage with a scriptable, resumable command-line tool. What should you use?",["AzCopy","Azure Storage Explorer's drag and drop","The portal upload blade","Robocopy to an SMB share"],0,"AzCopy is the command-line tool for high-throughput, resumable copies to and between storage accounts. Portal uploads and drag-and-drop do not suit terabytes or automation.","Manage data with AzCopy and Storage Explorer",[null,"Storage Explorer's drag and drop is interactive, not scriptable, and is poorly suited to 2 TB automated copies.","Portal uploads are meant for small files and cannot be scripted or resumed for terabytes of data.","Robocopy copies to SMB file shares, not to Blob Storage."]],["adm35",0,2,"Users in a branch office cannot mount an Azure file share, although it works from Azure VMs. What is the most likely cause?",["The share is using the Hot tier","The storage account uses ZRS","The branch ISP blocks outbound TCP 445","Share snapshots are enabled"],2,"SMB uses TCP 445, which many ISPs and networks block outbound. Tier, redundancy and snapshots do not stop mounting.","Configure Azure Files",["Access tiers affect cost and performance, not whether a client can open an SMB connection to the share.","ZRS only controls how data is replicated inside Azure and does not affect mounting from a branch office.",null,"Share snapshots are read-only point-in-time copies and do not stop clients from mounting the share."]],["adm36",0,2,"Domain users should access an Azure file share with their AD credentials and NTFS permissions. What is the correct order of work?",["Assign Reader on the storage account and share the account key","Create a SAS token per user and map it as a network drive","Enable large file shares and set only NTFS permissions on the share root","Enable identity-based auth, assign share roles, then set NTFS ACLs"],3,"Identity-based access needs an identity source (AD DS, Entra Domain Services or Entra Kerberos), share-level roles such as Storage File Data SMB Share Contributor, then Windows ACLs. The account key bypasses identity.","Configure identity-based access for Azure Files",["Reader on the account plus the account key gives everyone full, shared access and bypasses AD credentials and NTFS permissions.","SAS tokens cannot be used to mount an SMB file share and do not carry the user's AD identity.","Large file shares only raise capacity limits, and NTFS ACLs alone do nothing until identity-based auth and share-level roles are in place.",null]],["adm37",0,2,"A developer deleted a container by mistake. Which setting, if enabled beforehand, allows it to be restored?",["Container soft delete","Blob snapshots","Change feed","Lifecycle management"],0,"Container soft delete retains deleted containers for the retention period. Blob snapshots protect individual blobs, and change feed only logs changes.","Configure data protection",[null,"Blob snapshots protect individual blobs, and they are lost along with the container when it is deleted.","Change feed records a log of changes; it does not keep deleted containers.","Lifecycle management moves or deletes blobs by age; it cannot restore anything."]],["adm38",0,2,"A security review found an access key was leaked. What should you do to invalidate it while keeping apps running?",["Enable soft delete and wait for the leaked key to expire on its own","Switch apps to the other key, then regenerate the leaked key","Delete the storage account and restore it","Change the account's redundancy to GRS"],1,"Two keys exist so you can move apps to the unaffected key and then regenerate the leaked one. Keys do not expire, and redundancy is unrelated.","Manage access keys",["Storage account keys never expire on their own, so waiting leaves the leaked key usable; soft delete protects data, not keys.",null,"Deleting the account destroys the data and every app connection, which is drastic and does not keep apps running.","Redundancy settings control replication and have no effect on which keys are valid."]],["adm39",0,2,"Different departments share one storage account but each must use its own customer-managed key for its containers. What should you use?",["Separate access tiers per container","Stored access policies","Encryption scopes","Object replication rules"],2,"Encryption scopes let you choose a key per container or blob in the same account. Stored access policies control SAS, not encryption.","Configure encryption",["Access tiers set cost and availability for blobs; they have nothing to do with which encryption key is used.","Stored access policies control SAS tokens, not encryption keys.",null,"Object replication copies blobs to another account; it does not assign encryption keys per container."]],["adm40",0,3,"You redeploy an ARM template in complete mode to a resource group that contains an extra storage account not in the template. What happens to that account?",["It is deleted","It is left unchanged","It is locked as ReadOnly","It is moved to a new resource group"],0,"Complete mode deletes resources in the resource group that are not defined in the template. Incremental mode, the default, would leave it alone.","Automate deployment with ARM templates or Bicep",[null,"Left unchanged is what incremental mode (the default) does; complete mode removes resources that are not in the template.","Deployments never apply locks unless the template defines one; complete mode deletes extra resources instead.","Deployments do not move resources between resource groups; moves are a separate operation."]],["adm41",0,3,"An admin has an existing ARM JSON template and wants to maintain it as Bicep from now on. Which command helps?",["az bicep build","az bicep decompile","az deployment group export","az group create --template"],1,"`az bicep decompile` converts ARM JSON to a Bicep file, which you then review. `az bicep build` goes the other way, from Bicep to JSON.","Convert an ARM template to Bicep",["az bicep build compiles Bicep into ARM JSON, the opposite of what is needed.",null,"Export produces a template from existing resources, not a Bicep version of your existing JSON file.","az group create creates a resource group and does not take a template or convert one."]],["adm42",0,3,"A resource group was built by hand in the portal, and you need a template to recreate it in another subscription. What is the quickest starting point?",["Create a new blank template in VS Code","Copy the activity log to a JSON file","Use Export template on the resource group","Run az deployment group what-if"],2,"Export template generates an ARM template (and Bicep in the portal) from existing resources, which you then clean up and parameterize. What-if only previews a deployment.","Export a deployment as a template",["Writing a template from scratch is slow and easy to get wrong when the resources already exist to export from.","The activity log records operations, not a deployable description of the resources.",null,"What-if previews the effect of a template you already have; it does not generate one."]],["adm43",0,3,"A Bicep file must use a different VM size in dev and prod without editing the file. What should you add?",["A variable with a fixed size value","An output that returns the VM size after the deployment finishes","A resource dependency on the VNet","A parameter for the VM size, set per environment"],3,"Parameters take values at deployment time, for example from a parameters file per environment. Variables are computed inside the file, and outputs return values after deployment.","Interpret and modify templates",["A variable is computed inside the file, so changing the size would still mean editing the file for each environment.","Outputs return values after deployment finishes; they cannot feed a different size into the deployment.","A dependency only controls deployment order and does not change the VM size.",null]],["adm44",0,3,"Before running a template against production, you want to see which resources would be created, changed or deleted. What should you use?",["The what-if operation","Validate-only mode in Storage Explorer","The Activity log","An Azure Policy Audit effect"],0,"`az deployment group what-if` (or -WhatIf in PowerShell) previews changes without deploying. The activity log only shows what already happened.","Deploy resources by using templates",[null,"Storage Explorer manages storage data and has no validate-only mode for ARM or Bicep deployments.","The activity log only shows operations that already happened, not what a deployment would change.","An Audit policy reports compliance of resources; it does not preview a template's creates, changes and deletes."]],["adm45",0,3,"A two-VM web tier must stay available if one entire datacenter in the region fails. What should you deploy?",["Both VMs in one availability set","Both VMs in separate availability zones","Both VMs on a dedicated host","One VM with Premium SSD disks"],1,"Availability zones are physically separate datacenters, so VMs in different zones survive a datacenter outage (99.99% SLA). Availability sets only spread VMs across racks in one datacenter.","Configure VM availability",["An availability set spreads VMs across racks in one datacenter, so losing that datacenter takes down both VMs.",null,"A dedicated host is a single physical server, which is a single point of failure rather than protection.","Premium SSD improves disk performance and the single-VM SLA, but one VM cannot survive the loss of its datacenter."]],["adm46",0,3,"VMs in an availability set are spread across fault domains and update domains. What do update domains protect against?",["Loss of power or networking to a single server rack","Loss of an entire region","Planned maintenance rebooting all VMs at once","Accidental deletion of a VM"],2,"Update domains ensure planned maintenance reboots only one group at a time. Fault domains protect against rack-level hardware, power or network failure.","Configure VM availability",["Protection against a rack losing power or networking is what fault domains provide, not update domains.","Neither update domains nor fault domains protect against a region outage; that needs a second region.",null,"Update domains group VMs for maintenance; they do nothing to prevent someone deleting a VM."]],["adm47",0,3,"You try to resize a running VM, but the size you need is not listed. What should you do?",["Delete the VM and recreate it from the marketplace","Change the OS disk to Ultra disk","Move the VM to another resource group","Stop (deallocate) the VM and check the sizes again"],3,"If a size is not available on the current hardware cluster, deallocating the VM lets Azure place it on hardware that supports the new size.","Manage VM sizes",["Recreating the VM is unnecessary, because deallocating it usually exposes the sizes supported on other hardware.","Ultra Disk is a data disk type that cannot be an OS disk, and disk type does not decide which VM sizes are listed.","Resource group membership does not affect which sizes the hardware cluster supports.",null]],["adm48",0,3,"A VM's temporary disk, OS disk and caches must be encrypted end to end on the host, without running BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the guest. What should you enable?",["Encryption at host","Azure Disk Encryption","A storage account firewall","Trusted launch only"],0,"Encryption at host encrypts temp disks and caches at the VM host, with no in-guest agent. Azure Disk Encryption uses BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the OS.","Configure VM disk encryption",[null,"Azure Disk Encryption runs BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the guest OS, which the requirement rules out.","A storage account firewall controls network access to storage; it does not encrypt VM disks or caches.","Trusted launch adds secure boot and vTPM protections; it does not encrypt temp disks and caches on the host."]],["adm49",0,3,"A database VM needs the lowest disk latency and settings for IOPS and throughput that can be changed on the fly, for its data disk. Which disk type fits best?",["Standard HDD","Ultra Disk or Premium SSD v2","Standard SSD","An ephemeral OS disk"],1,"Ultra Disk and Premium SSD v2 offer sub-millisecond latency and adjustable IOPS and throughput for data disks. Neither can be used as an OS disk; ephemeral OS disks are lost on deallocation.","Manage VM disks",["Standard HDD has the highest latency and lowest performance, and its IOPS cannot be tuned on the fly.",null,"Standard SSD is more consistent than HDD but does not offer sub-millisecond latency or adjustable IOPS and throughput.","Ephemeral OS disks hold only the OS on local storage, are lost on deallocation and cannot serve as a database data disk."]],["adm50",0,3,"A VM must move from West US to North Europe with minimal manual rebuild. Which approach is supported?",["Change the region field in the VM's properties","Move the VM into a resource group whose location is North Europe","Use Azure Resource Mover or Site Recovery to move it","Detach the NIC and attach it to a North Europe VNet"],2,"Cross-region moves need Resource Mover or Site Recovery replication. Moving to a resource group in another region does not change the VM's region.","Move VMs",["A VM's region is fixed at creation and is not an editable property.","A resource group's location only stores its metadata; moving a VM into it does not change the VM's region.",null,"A NIC must be in the same region as its VM, so it cannot be attached to a VNet in another region to move the VM."]],["adm51",0,3,"A web tier must add VM instances automatically when average CPU exceeds 70% and remove them when it drops. What should you deploy?",["An availability set with 10 VMs spread across fault domains","A single larger VM size","A proximity placement group","A Virtual Machine Scale Set with autoscale rules"],3,"Scale sets with autoscale add and remove instances based on metrics such as CPU. An availability set does not scale automatically.","Deploy and configure scale sets",["An availability set improves resilience, but it never adds or removes VMs based on CPU load.","A single larger VM still has a fixed capacity and does not remove instances when load drops.","A proximity placement group reduces latency between VMs by placing them close together; it does not scale anything.",null]],["adm52",0,3,"A company needs a private container registry replicated to three regions with private endpoints. Which ACR tier is required?",["Premium","Basic","Standard","Any tier with admin user enabled"],0,"Geo-replication and private link are Premium features. Basic and Standard differ mainly in storage and throughput.","Manage Azure Container Registry",[null,"Basic does not support geo-replication or private endpoints.","Standard offers more storage and throughput than Basic but still lacks geo-replication and private link.","The admin user is a single shared login and has no effect on which features a tier supports."]],["adm53",0,3,"A nightly batch job runs in one container for 20 minutes and must not restart after it exits successfully. What is the simplest option?",["An App Service plan with Always On","Azure Container Instances with restart policy OnFailure","A Virtual Machine Scale Set with one instance","Azure Container Apps with the minimum replica count set to 3"],1,"ACI runs a container group on demand, and OnFailure restarts only if the container exits with an error. The other options keep compute running permanently.","Provision Azure Container Instances",["An App Service plan with Always On keeps compute running and billed all day for a 20-minute job.",null,"A scale set keeps at least one VM running, so you pay for idle compute and must manage the VM.","Container Apps with a minimum of three replicas keeps three copies running all the time, the opposite of a short batch run."]],["adm54",0,3,"A microservice should scale from zero to many replicas based on HTTP traffic and split traffic between two versions. Which service fits?",["Azure Container Instances","A single VM running Docker","Azure Container Apps","Azure Container Registry Tasks"],2,"Container Apps provides revisions with traffic splitting and KEDA-based scaling, including scale to zero. ACI has no built-in autoscaling or revisions.","Manage Azure Container Apps",["Container Instances run container groups on demand but have no built-in autoscaling or revision traffic splitting.","A single Docker VM needs you to build scaling and traffic splitting yourself and cannot scale to zero.",null,"ACR Tasks build and patch container images; they do not run or scale a microservice."]],["adm55",0,3,"An App Service app needs deployment slots so new versions can be tested and swapped into production. What is the minimum plan tier?",["Free","Shared","Basic","Standard"],3,"Deployment slots start at the Standard tier (Premium and Isolated offer more slots). Free, Shared and Basic have no slots.","Configure deployment slots",["The Free tier is for testing and has no deployment slots.","The Shared tier runs apps on shared compute and has no deployment slots.","Basic adds dedicated compute and custom domains but still has no deployment slots.",null]],["adm56",0,3,"A web app's App Service plan is at 90% memory and you need a larger instance size. Which action is this?",["Scale up the App Service plan","Scale out to more instances","Add a deployment slot","Enable VNet integration"],0,"Scaling up moves the plan to a bigger size or tier with more memory and CPU. Scaling out adds more instances of the same size.","Configure scaling for App Service",[null,"Scaling out adds more instances of the same size, which does not give each instance more memory.","A deployment slot is a separate app instance for staging and does not add memory to production.","VNet integration provides outbound access to a virtual network and does not change instance size."]],["adm57",0,3,"After swapping staging into production, the production app started using the staging database connection string. How do you prevent this next time?",["Store the connection string directly in the application code","Mark the connection string as a deployment slot setting","Use a Free tier plan for staging","Disable autoswap on production"],1,"Slot settings stick to their slot and do not move during a swap. Normal app settings and connection strings swap with the content.","Configure deployment slots",["Hard-coding the connection string would travel with the code during a swap and makes secrets harder to manage.",null,"The Free tier has no deployment slots at all, and the plan tier has nothing to do with which settings swap.","Autoswap only triggers the swap automatically; a manual swap would still move the staging connection string."]],["adm58",0,3,"You are mapping www.contoso.com to an App Service app. Which DNS record is typically created for this subdomain?",["An MX record pointing to the app","A PTR record for the app's IP","A CNAME record to the app's azurewebsites.net name","An NS record pointing to the App Service name servers for the app"],2,"A subdomain such as www usually maps with a CNAME to the default host name (plus a TXT asuid record for verification). MX is for mail and PTR for reverse lookups.","Map a custom DNS name",["MX records route email for a domain and cannot point a web host name to an app.","PTR records are for reverse lookups from an IP address to a name, not for mapping a host name to an app.",null,"NS records delegate a whole zone to name servers; App Service does not host DNS zones for individual apps."]],["adm59",0,3,"A web app must call a database that is reachable only on a private IP inside a VNet. Which App Service feature provides this outbound access?",["A private endpoint on the web app","A custom domain with TLS","Access restrictions","VNet integration"],3,"VNet integration lets the app make outbound calls into the VNet. A private endpoint gives inbound private access to the app, and access restrictions filter inbound traffic.","Configure networking for App Service",["A private endpoint gives clients inbound private access to the app; it does not let the app call into the VNet.","A custom domain with TLS changes how users reach the app and has no effect on its outbound connectivity.","Access restrictions filter inbound traffic to the app; they do not provide outbound access to private IPs.",null]],["adm60",0,3,"An App Service app needs automatic scheduled backups of its content and configuration. What must be true?",["The plan tier must support backups (Basic or higher)","The app must run in a container","A Recovery Services vault must be in the same region","The app must have a deployment slot"],0,"App Service backups are built into supported plan tiers (Basic and above) and store data in a storage account. They do not use a Recovery Services vault or require containers or slots.","Configure backup for App Service",[null,"App Service backups work for code-based apps too; running in a container is not required.","App Service backups are stored in a storage account, not a Recovery Services vault.","Backups do not depend on deployment slots; a single production app can be backed up."]],["adm61",0,3,"A scale set runs a stateless app. During scale-in, you want the newest VMs removed first. What should you configure?",["An availability set","A scale-in policy of NewestVM","Manual scaling only","A proximity placement group"],1,"Scale-in policies (Default, NewestVM, OldestVM) choose which instances are removed. Availability sets and placement groups do not control scale-in order.","Deploy and configure scale sets",["Availability sets place VMs across fault and update domains; they do not decide which scale set instances are removed.",null,"Manual scaling only changes who decides the instance count; it does not choose the newest VMs to remove.","A proximity placement group controls physical placement for low latency, not scale-in order."]],["adm62",0,4,"You are planning a subnet for 27 VMs and want to use as little address space as possible. What is the smallest subnet that fits?",["/28","/26","/27","/29"],2,"Azure reserves 5 addresses in every subnet. A /27 has 32 addresses, leaving exactly 27 usable. A /28 leaves only 11, and a /26 fits but wastes space.","Configure virtual networks",["A /28 has 16 addresses, and after Azure reserves 5 only 11 remain, which is too few for 27 VMs.","A /26 leaves 59 usable addresses, so it fits but wastes space compared with a /27.",null,"A /29 has 8 addresses and only 3 usable after Azure's reservations."]],["adm63",0,4,"VNet A is peered with VNet B, and VNet B with VNet C. VMs in A cannot reach VMs in C. What is the reason?",["Global peering is required between regions","NSGs block all peered traffic by default","VNet peering is not transitive","The address spaces must be the same"],2,"Peering is non-transitive, so A needs its own peering with C, or traffic must go through an NVA or gateway in B with user-defined routes. Default NSG rules allow VirtualNetwork traffic.","Configure VNet peering",["Global peering connects VNets in different regions, but it is still not transitive, and the question does not say the VNets are in different regions.","The default AllowVnetInBound rule permits traffic between peered VNets, so NSGs are not blocking it by default.",null,"Peered VNets must have non-overlapping address spaces, so identical address spaces would prevent peering."]],["adm64",0,4,"Spoke VNets must use the hub VNet's VPN gateway to reach on-premises. Which peering settings are needed?",["Allow gateway transit on the hub, Use remote gateways on the spokes","Use remote gateways on the hub, Allow gateway transit on the spokes","Allow forwarded traffic only on both sides","A second VPN gateway in each spoke"],0,"The hub peering allows gateway transit and each spoke peering uses remote gateways. Reversing them does not work, and a spoke with remote gateways cannot have its own gateway.","Configure VNet peering",[null,"This reverses the settings: the hub must allow gateway transit and the spokes must use the remote gateway.","Allow forwarded traffic permits traffic that did not originate in the peered VNet, but it does not let spokes use the hub's gateway.","A gateway in each spoke is costly and unnecessary, and a spoke that uses remote gateways cannot have its own gateway."]],["adm65",0,4,"All internet-bound traffic from a subnet must pass through a firewall appliance at 10.0.1.4. What should you create?",["An NSG outbound rule whose destination is 10.0.1.4","A UDR for 0.0.0.0/0 with next hop Virtual appliance","A service endpoint for Microsoft.Network on the subnet","A private DNS zone for the firewall"],1,"A user-defined route with 0.0.0.0/0 and next hop type Virtual appliance sends traffic to the NVA. NSGs only allow or deny traffic; they cannot redirect it.","Configure user-defined routes",["NSG rules only allow or deny traffic; they cannot change the next hop to send traffic through a firewall.",null,"Service endpoints route traffic to specific Azure services over the backbone; there is no endpoint that forces traffic through an appliance.","A private DNS zone only resolves names and cannot change how packets are routed."]],["adm66",0,4,"An NSG has an inbound rule allowing TCP 3389 at priority 300 and a rule denying TCP 3389 at priority 200. What happens to RDP traffic?",["It is allowed, because allow rules always win over deny rules","It is allowed, because 300 is higher","It depends on the NIC NSG only","It is denied, because priority 200 is processed first"],3,"Lower numbers are processed first and processing stops at the first match, so the priority-200 deny applies.","Configure network security groups",["NSGs do not favor allow rules; the rule with the lowest priority number that matches wins.","A higher priority number means lower precedence, so 300 is processed after 200.","The question concerns rules in one NSG, whose priority order decides the result regardless of any NIC NSG.",null]],["adm67",0,4,"An NSG on a subnet allows inbound HTTPS, but a separate NSG on a VM's NIC has only the default rules. Can internet clients reach HTTPS on the VM?",["No, because the NIC NSG's default DenyAllInBound blocks it","Yes, because the subnet NSG overrides the NIC NSG","Yes, because default rules allow internet traffic","No, because two NSGs cannot be combined"],0,"Inbound traffic must be allowed by both the subnet NSG and the NIC NSG. The NIC's default rules deny inbound internet traffic.","Configure network security groups",[null,"Neither NSG overrides the other; inbound traffic must be allowed by both the subnet NSG and the NIC NSG.","The default rules allow VirtualNetwork and AzureLoadBalancer traffic but deny inbound internet traffic.","Subnet and NIC NSGs can be combined; traffic is evaluated against both."]],["adm68",0,4,"Web servers and database servers share a subnet. You want NSG rules that refer to 'web' and 'db' roles instead of IP addresses. What should you use?",["Service tags","Route tables","Application security groups","Private DNS zones"],2,"Application security groups let you group NICs by role and use those groups as sources and destinations in NSG rules. Service tags represent Azure services, not your VMs.","Configure application security groups",["Service tags represent groups of Azure service IP ranges, such as Storage or AzureLoadBalancer, not your own VMs by role.","Route tables control where traffic goes next; they cannot group VMs for NSG rules.",null,"Private DNS zones resolve names inside VNets and cannot be used as NSG sources or destinations."]],["adm69",0,4,"Admins need RDP and SSH to VMs that have no public IPs, from a browser over TLS. What should you deploy?",["A Standard Load Balancer with inbound NAT rules for RDP and SSH","A public IP on each VM with an NSG","A point-to-site VPN for each admin","Azure Bastion in a subnet named AzureBastionSubnet"],3,"Azure Bastion provides browser-based RDP and SSH over TLS and must be deployed into AzureBastionSubnet. Public IPs expose the VMs directly.","Configure Azure Bastion",["Inbound NAT rules on a load balancer still expose RDP and SSH ports to the internet and do not provide browser-based access over TLS.","Public IPs expose each VM's management ports directly, which the requirement for no public IPs rules out.","A point-to-site VPN gives network access but needs a client and gateway setup per admin, not browser-based RDP and SSH.",null]],["adm70",0,4,"On-premises users connected by VPN must reach a storage account on a private IP, and public access must be disabled. What should you use?",["A service endpoint on the subnet","A private endpoint with a private DNS zone","A storage firewall IP rule for the VPN range","A user-defined route to the storage public IP"],1,"A private endpoint gives the storage account a private IP in the VNet that on-premises clients can reach over VPN, with private DNS for the privatelink name. Service endpoints only work from the VNet's own subnets.","Configure private endpoints",["Service endpoints only work for traffic from subnets in the VNet, not for on-premises clients over VPN, and still use the public endpoint.",null,"An IP rule for the VPN range still relies on the public endpoint, which must be disabled.","Routing to the storage public IP still uses the public endpoint, which contradicts disabling public access."]],["adm71",0,4,"VMs in a VNet should get DNS names like vm1.corp.internal automatically as they are created. What should you configure?",["A public DNS zone with alias records for each VM","A CNAME record for each VM","A private DNS zone linked with auto-registration","A custom DNS server installed on each VM in the VNet"],2,"Private DNS zones linked with auto-registration create and remove A records for VMs in the VNet. Public zones are for internet names.","Configure Azure DNS",["Public DNS zones hold internet-facing names; they cannot auto-register VMs from a VNet.","Creating a CNAME for each VM is manual work and cannot happen automatically as VMs are created.",null,"Running a custom DNS server on every VM is unnecessary and would not register the VMs' names by itself."]],["adm72",0,4,"You bought contoso.com at a third-party registrar and created a public zone in Azure DNS. Names still do not resolve through Azure. What is missing?",["An A record for the zone apex that points to your web server","A private DNS zone link","A reverse lookup zone","Registrar NS records pointing to Azure DNS"],3,"Delegation requires the registrar's NS records to point to the Azure DNS name servers shown on the zone. Records in the zone are ignored until then.","Configure Azure DNS",["An apex A record answers queries only after the zone is delegated; until the registrar points to Azure DNS, queries never reach the zone.","A private DNS zone link resolves names inside a VNet and has no effect on public internet resolution.","Reverse lookup zones map IP addresses to names and do not fix forward resolution of contoso.com.",null]],["adm73",0,4,"An internal line-of-business app on three VMs needs a single private IP that spreads TCP traffic and skips unhealthy VMs. What should you deploy?",["An internal Standard Load Balancer with a health probe","A public Basic Load Balancer with a floating IP on each VM","Azure DNS round robin","A NAT gateway"],0,"An internal load balancer uses a private frontend IP, and health probes remove failed backends. DNS round robin has no health checks, and a NAT gateway handles outbound traffic only.","Configure an internal load balancer",[null,"A public load balancer exposes a public IP, which is wrong for an internal app, and the Basic SKU is retired.","DNS round robin has no health checks, so clients keep getting the addresses of unhealthy VMs.","A NAT gateway provides outbound internet access for a subnet and does not distribute inbound traffic."]],["adm74",0,4,"Backend VMs behind a Standard Load Balancer are running, but clients get no response. The health probe shows all backends down. What should you check first?",["The load balancer's SKU","NSGs allow the AzureLoadBalancer probe and the port listens","The public DNS zone's TTL for the frontend record name","Whether the VMs have availability set membership"],1,"Health probes come from the AzureLoadBalancer service tag and must reach a listening port. If NSGs block them or the app is not listening, all backends are marked down.","Troubleshoot load balancing",["The SKU is already Standard, and changing it would not explain why every health probe is failing.",null,"DNS TTL affects how long clients cache the frontend address; it has no effect on backend health probes.","Availability set membership does not decide whether health probes succeed."]],["adm75",0,4,"A VM cannot reach another VM on TCP 1433. You want to know which NSG rule is dropping the traffic. Which Network Watcher tool is quickest?",["Packet capture on both VMs","Connection Monitor dashboards","IP flow verify","Traffic analytics"],2,"IP flow verify tests a specific 5-tuple and names the NSG rule that allows or denies it. Packet capture shows traffic but not which rule dropped it.","Troubleshoot network connectivity",["Packet capture records traffic but does not name the NSG rule that dropped it, and it takes longer to set up and analyze.","Connection Monitor tracks reachability and latency over time; it is not the quickest way to identify a specific NSG rule.",null,"Traffic analytics summarizes flow logs over time and needs flow logging configured first, so it is not a quick per-flow check."]],["adm76",0,4,"Traffic from a VM is not reaching the expected firewall. Which tool shows the route and next hop Azure will use for a destination?",["IP flow verify","Azure Advisor","Activity log","Network Watcher next hop"],3,"Next hop reports the next hop type and IP and the route table in effect. IP flow verify checks NSG rules, not routing.","Troubleshoot network connectivity",["IP flow verify checks whether NSG rules allow or deny a flow; it does not report routes or next hops.","Azure Advisor gives best-practice recommendations, not the route in effect for a destination.","The activity log records control-plane operations and does not show packet routing.",null]],["adm77",0,4,"You need a public IP that is zone-redundant, static and closed to inbound traffic unless an NSG allows it. Which SKU fits?",["Standard","Basic","Dynamic Basic","Any SKU with a DNS label"],0,"Standard public IPs are always static, can be zone-redundant and are secure by default. The Basic SKU is retired and lacked zone redundancy.","Configure public IP addresses",[null,"The Basic SKU is retired, was open by default and did not support zone redundancy.","Dynamic Basic addresses change when the resource is deallocated, so they are neither static nor zone-redundant.","A DNS label only adds a name to the IP and does not make it static, zone-redundant or closed by default."]],["adm78",0,4,"A storage account should accept traffic only from one subnet, and the storage account can keep its public endpoint. What is the simplest option?",["A private endpoint for the storage account in the subnet","A service endpoint plus a storage firewall VNet rule","A user-defined route with next hop None","An NSG with the Storage service tag on the account"],1,"Service endpoints with a VNet rule restrict the public endpoint to the subnet, which is simpler and cheaper than a private endpoint. NSGs cannot be attached to storage accounts.","Configure service endpoints",["A private endpoint works, but it adds cost and DNS setup, while the question asks for the simplest option with the public endpoint kept.",null,"A route with next hop None drops traffic; it cannot allow a subnet and block everyone else at the storage account.","NSGs attach to subnets and NICs, not storage accounts, so they cannot filter who reaches the account."]],["adm79",0,5,"An admin wants a platform-level record of who deleted a resource group last week. Where should they look first?",["VM insights","The Activity log","Storage analytics logs","Connection Monitor"],1,"The Activity log records control-plane operations such as deletes, with the caller, for 90 days. VM insights and Connection Monitor focus on performance and connectivity.","Monitor resources in Azure",["VM insights shows performance and dependency data for VMs, not who deleted a resource group.",null,"Storage analytics logs record data-plane requests to a storage account, not resource group deletions.","Connection Monitor tracks network reachability and latency and records no management operations."]],["adm80",0,5,"Resource logs from a Key Vault must be kept and queried with KQL alongside other logs. What should you configure?",["A diagnostic setting to a Log Analytics workspace","An action group with an email receiver for the vault owners","A metric alert on vault availability","A resource lock on the Key Vault"],0,"Resource logs are only collected when a diagnostic setting routes them to a destination such as Log Analytics. Alerts and action groups do not store logs.","Configure log settings",[null,"An action group defines who is notified when an alert fires; it does not collect or store logs.","A metric alert watches a metric value and neither collects resource logs nor lets you query them with KQL.","A lock prevents changes or deletion of the vault and has no effect on log collection."]],["adm81",0,5,"Which KQL query returns the count of heartbeats per computer over the last hour?",["Heartbeat | project Computer, TimeGenerated | take 1h","Heartbeat | render timechart by Computer","Heartbeat | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) | summarize count() by Computer","Heartbeat | sort by Computer asc, TimeGenerated desc | limit 1h hours"],2,"`where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)` filters the time range and `summarize count() by Computer` groups the results. take and limit expect a number of rows, not a time.","Query and analyze logs",["take expects a number of rows, not a time span, and project alone does not count heartbeats per computer.","render only chooses a chart type and does not filter to the last hour or count rows.",null,"limit expects a row count, so '1h hours' is invalid, and sorting does not count heartbeats."]],["adm82",0,5,"Alerts should keep firing during a planned weekend maintenance window, but nobody should be notified. What should you create?",["A new action group with no receivers attached to every alert rule","A ReadOnly lock on the alert rules","A budget alert","An alert processing rule that suppresses notifications"],3,"Alert processing rules can suppress action groups for a schedule while alerts still fire and are recorded. Editing action groups or locking rules is clumsy and error-prone.","Configure alert processing rules",["An empty action group attached to every rule is error-prone and must be undone later; alert processing rules handle schedules cleanly.","A ReadOnly lock stops edits to the alert rules but does not stop notifications from being sent.","A budget alert watches spending and has no effect on other alerts' notifications.",null]],["adm83",0,5,"When a VM's CPU alert fires, the on-call team should get an SMS and a runbook should restart a service. Where are these responses defined?",["In an action group","In the diagnostic setting","In the Log Analytics workspace","In a data collection rule"],0,"Action groups hold notification and action receivers such as SMS, email, webhooks and runbooks. Diagnostic settings and DCRs control data collection.","Configure alerts and action groups",[null,"A diagnostic setting routes logs and metrics to a destination; it does not send SMS or run runbooks.","A Log Analytics workspace stores and queries data; it does not define alert responses.","A data collection rule defines what the Azure Monitor Agent collects, not what happens when an alert fires."]],["adm84",0,5,"You want performance trends and a dependency map for a group of VMs. What should you enable?",["Network Watcher packet capture","Azure Advisor","VM insights with the Azure Monitor Agent","Blob storage analytics"],2,"VM insights uses the Azure Monitor Agent and a data collection rule to show performance and, optionally, process dependencies. Advisor gives recommendations, not trends.","Configure Azure Monitor insights",["Packet capture records network traffic on one VM; it gives neither performance trends nor a dependency map.","Advisor makes recommendations but does not chart VM performance over time or map dependencies.",null,"Storage analytics covers storage account requests and has nothing to do with VM performance."]],["adm85",0,5,"You need to back up Azure VMs and Azure file shares. Which vault should you create?",["A Backup vault","A Key Vault","A storage account with GRS","A Recovery Services vault"],3,"Recovery Services vaults protect Azure VMs and Azure Files. Backup vaults cover workloads such as managed disks, blobs and PostgreSQL.","Create a Recovery Services vault",["A Backup vault protects workloads such as managed disks, blobs and Azure Database for PostgreSQL, not Azure VMs and Azure Files.","Key Vault stores secrets, keys and certificates and cannot hold backups.","A storage account with GRS is not a backup service and has no backup policies or recovery points.",null]],["adm86",0,5,"A VM backup must be restorable in the paired region if the primary region is down. What must be set on the vault before backups are taken?",["LRS storage redundancy","GRS redundancy with Cross Region Restore enabled","A second vault in the same region","Soft delete disabled"],1,"Cross Region Restore requires a GRS vault with the feature enabled. LRS keeps data in one region only, and soft delete does not affect region restores.","Configure backup and restore",["LRS keeps backup data in one region, so it cannot be restored in the paired region.",null,"A second vault in the same region fails along with the primary region during a regional outage.","Disabling soft delete lowers protection against deleted backups and does nothing for cross-region restore."]],["adm87",0,5,"A user deleted one spreadsheet from a VM protected by Azure Backup yesterday. What is the fastest way to recover just that file?",["Restore the entire VM to a new VM","Fail over the VM with Site Recovery to the paired region","Use File Recovery to mount the recovery point","Restore the managed disk and swap the OS disk"],2,"File Recovery mounts a recovery point as a drive through a script so you can copy single files. Restoring or failing over whole VMs takes far longer.","Perform restore operations",["Restoring the whole VM to recover one file is slow and creates a new VM you then need to clean up.","Site Recovery failover moves the whole workload to another region; it is for disasters, not single-file recovery.",null,"Restoring a disk and swapping the OS disk replaces the whole disk and takes far longer than mounting a recovery point."]],["adm88",0,5,"A critical VM needs backups every 4 hours. What should you use?",["An Enhanced backup policy with hourly frequency","A Standard policy with daily backups","A lifecycle management rule","A snapshot taken manually each day"],0,"Enhanced policies support multiple backups per day (hourly schedules). Standard policies allow one backup a day.","Configure backup policies",[null,"A Standard policy allows only one backup a day, which cannot meet a 4-hour schedule.","Lifecycle management rules act on blobs in storage accounts and cannot back up a VM.","A daily manual snapshot runs only once a day and is not a managed backup schedule."]],["adm89",0,5,"You must prove your disaster recovery plan for replicated VMs works without affecting production or ongoing replication. What should you run?",["A planned failover","An on-demand backup","A test failover into an isolated VNet","A restore to an alternate location"],2,"A test failover in Site Recovery creates VMs in an isolated network while replication continues. A planned failover moves production.","Configure Azure Site Recovery",["A planned failover moves production to the secondary region, which is exactly the impact the drill must avoid.","An on-demand backup protects data but does not test that replicated VMs can start in the recovery region.",null,"Restoring from backup to another location tests backups, not Site Recovery replication and failover."]],["adm90",0,5,"After a Site Recovery failover to the secondary region, the primary region is healthy again. What should happen before failing back?",["Delete the recovery plan and create it again","Re-protect the VMs to reverse replication","Disable replication permanently","Convert the vault to a Backup vault"],1,"Re-protect starts replication in the reverse direction so you can fail back later. Deleting plans or disabling replication loses protection.","Perform failover to a secondary region",["Deleting and recreating the recovery plan does not restart replication, and you would lose the plan's configuration.",null,"Disabling replication leaves the VMs unprotected and makes a managed failback impossible.","Site Recovery runs from a Recovery Services vault; it cannot be converted to a Backup vault, and that would not reverse replication."]]]);
